NNAAMMEE
     ssaassyynnccdd — IPsec SA synchronization daemon for failover gateways

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssaassyynnccdd [--ddnnvv] [--cc _c_o_n_f_i_g_-_f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssaassyynnccdd daemon synchronizes IPsec SA and SPD information between a
     number of failover IPsec gateways.  The most typical scenario is to run
     ssaassyynnccdd on hosts also running isakmpd(8) or iked(8) and sharing a common
     IP address using carp(4).

     The daemon runs either in master or slave mode, in which the master
     tracks all local IPsec SA changes and sends this information along to all
     slaves so they will have the same data.

     When a slave connects, or reconnects, the master will transmit a snapshot
     of all its current IPsec SA and SPD information.

   FFaaiilloovveerr
     ssaassyynnccdd does not itself do any failover processing; the normal mode of
     operation is to track state changes on a specified carp(4) interface.
     Whenever it changes, ssaassyynnccdd will follow suit.  For debugging purposes,
     it is possible to "lock" the daemon to a particular state; see
     sasyncd.conf(5).

   ssaassyynnccdd ttoo ssaassyynnccdd ccoommmmuunniiccaattiioonn
     As ssaassyynnccdd will transmit IPsec SA key and policy information over a net‐
     work not guaranteed to be private, ssaassyynnccdd messages are protected using
     AES and SHA.  The shared key used for the encryption must be specified in
     _/_e_t_c_/_s_a_s_y_n_c_d_._c_o_n_f.  See sasyncd.conf(5) for more information.

   SSAA rreeppllaayy ccoouunntteerrss
     For SAs with replay protection enabled, such as those created by
     isakmpd(8), the ssaassyynnccdd hosts must have pfsync(4) enabled to synchronize
     the in-kernel SA replay counters.  Without this replay counter synchro‐
     nization the IPsec packets a host sends after failover will not be ac‐
     cepted by the remote VPN endpoint.

     In most redundancy setups pfsync(4) is likely already activated to syn‐
     chronize pf(4) states.  See pfsync(4) for more information.

     The options are as follows:

     --cc _c_o_n_f_i_g_-_f_i_l_e
             If given, the --cc option specifies an alternate configuration file
             instead of _/_e_t_c_/_s_a_s_y_n_c_d_._c_o_n_f.

     --dd      The --dd option causes the daemon to run in the foreground, logging
             to stderr.  Without this option, ssaassyynnccdd sends log messages to
             syslog(3).

     --nn      Configtest mode.  Only check the configuration file for validity.

     --vv      The --vv option increases the verbosity level of the daemon, used
             primarily for debugging.  This option may be specified several
             times.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/sasyncd.conf             The default ssaassyynnccdd configuration file.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     crypto(3), syslog(3), carp(4), ipsec(4), pfsync(4), sasyncd.conf(5),
     iked(8), isakmpd(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ssaassyynnccdd daemon first appeared in OpenBSD 3.8.  It was written in
     2004-2005 by Hakan Olsson, in part sponsored by Multicom Security AB,
     Sweden.

BBUUGGSS
     Due to the absence of a proper on the wire SA transfer protocol, ssaassyynnccdd
     only works if the peers share the same hardware architecture.

